  "_tierComment": "A mining laser's yield by its grade against the rock's tier (the system's Mk). Below the rock's tier it also needs more lasers on the rock to work well (crowd.kByGap); the two multiply to 1, so a lesser laser never out-mines a matching one however many are on it: a grade behind is slow work alone (about a third), two behind is hardly worth it; a grade ahead is a little better.",
  "tierEfficiency": {
    "0": 1,
    "1": 1.2,
    "2": 1.3,
    "-2": 0.2,
    "-1": 0.5
  },
  "_crowdComment": "Mining lasers sharing a rock (common/src/econ/mining.ts crowdFactor): each laser's extraction x base x g(n), n the mining lasers on the rock in the last `window` ticks (any ships). g peaks at 1 with k lasers on it and the total always rises, more slowly past k; `sharp` (2, 3, ...) climbs more steeply to k and gains less past it. Lasers below the rock's tier find k kByGap times as high. Rock types name their profile (types[t].crowd, else `default`); other weapons and the siphon get no crowd factor.",
  "crowd": {
    "window": 54,
    "default": "soft",
    "profiles": {
      "soft": {
        "k": 1.5,
        "sharp": 2,
        "base": 1.08
      },
      "hard": {
        "k": 5,
        "sharp": 3,
        "base": 0.4,
        "hint": true
      }
    },
    "kByGap": {
      "-1": 2,
      "-2": 5
    }
  },
  "_outliersComment": "Outliers: about one belt rock in 130 is 3.5-4.5x its size. Mining it (or blasting it) breaks it down through `stages` (its size as a fraction of the first), each a fresh smaller rock carrying the ore that's left, until the last breaks up. `common` of them are a lump of common ore: each ore's weight x its boost (unlisted ores x rare).",
